<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Introduction # {#introduction}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

*This section is non-normative.*

Today, websites that support federated sign-in pick a small number of large [=IDPs=] to show in
their sign-in flows, because each one takes space and needs a separate integration. Users whose
identity lives elsewhere (on a smaller provider, a custom domain, or a server they run themselves)
are left out. By contrast, email verification lets any mail server take part without the website
allow-listing it.

This specification lets the [=user agent=] act as an intermediary that removes the need for
allow-listing:

1. After the user signs in, an [=IDP=] **registers** the user's [=/handle=] by calling
    {{IdentityProvider/register()}}, with the user's permission.
1. An [=RP=] **requests** any registered [=IDP=] that belongs to a federation it accepts, by passing
    a {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} instead of a {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}:

    <div class="example" id="example-rp-request">
    ```js
    const credential = await navigator.credentials.get({
      identity: {
        providers: [{
          federation: "https://atproto.com",
          nonce: "..."
        }, {
          configURL: "https://idp.example/fedcm.json",
          clientId: "123"
        }]
      }
    });
    ```

    </div>

1. A user whose [=/handle=] isn't registered yet can **type it**, for example `@alice.example`. The
    user agent [=check a typed handle|resolves it=] to its
    [=/issuer=] and registers it ([=type your handle=]).

Both ways of registering a [=/handle=] run the same [=resolve a handle|resolution=] and add the same
[=/handle=] to the [=handle registry=].

This specification builds on the Handle Verification Protocol [[HVP]], which defines what a
[=/handle=] is, how it is resolved to the [=/issuer=] that the user has authorized to vouch for it,
and how the user agent obtains from that [=/issuer=] a proof that the user controls the
[=/handle=], an [=HVT+KB=], which the [=RP=] verifies. The [=/issuer=] doesn't learn which [=RP=]
the user signs in to.

<div class="example" id="example-idp-register">
After Alice signs in on `https://social.example`, the [=IDP=] sets its login status and registers
her handle:

```js
await navigator.login.setStatus("logged-in");
await IdentityProvider.register("@alice.example");
```

The user agent resolves `@alice.example`, through its DID document, to its [=/issuer=],
`https://social.example`. Its
[=/issuer metadata=], at `https://social.example/.well-known/handle-verification`, lists the
federations that its accounts belong to:

```json
{
  "issuer": "https://social.example",
  "issuance_endpoint": "https://social.example/hvp/issuance",
  "jwks_uri": "https://social.example/hvp/jwks",
  "federations": ["https://atproto.com"]
}
```

The [=well-known file=] of its site, at `https://social.example/.well-known/web-identity`, tells
the user agent where to fetch Alice's accounts and where to let her sign in:

```json
{
  "accounts_endpoint": "/accounts",
  "login_url": "/login"
}
```

And its {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} includes her [=/handle=] on her account:

```json
{
  "accounts": [{
    "id": "1234",
    "name": "Alice",
    "handle": "@alice.example",
    "picture": "https://social.example/alice.jpg"
  }]
}
```

</div>

This specification is written as a set of monkey patches to [[FEDCM]], shown like this:

<div class="monkeypatch">
A change to an algorithm or definition in another specification.

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Federations # {#federations}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

A federation, such as atproto, is identified by an `https:` URL: the URL that [=RPs=] pass as
{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} and that [=/issuers=] list in the
[=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] member of their [=/issuer metadata=]. The
<dfn>federation operator</dfn> is the party that controls the [=url/origin=] of that URL. It is
responsible for:

* Defining the federation: which accounts belong to it, and what [=RPs=] can do with them beyond
    verifying that the user controls their [=/handle=]. How [=RPs=] verify that is defined by
    [[HVP]], and is the same for every federation.
* Publishing [=operator metadata=] about the federation, such as its
    [=federation branding|branding=], which the user agent uses in its UI ([[#presentation]]).

The user agent doesn't need to understand the federation. It only reads the [=operator metadata=],
and matches the federation URL with the [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] of
[=/issuers=].

## Operator metadata ## {#operator-metadata-document}

A [=federation operator=] serves its <dfn>operator metadata</dfn> at the path
`/.well-known/web-identity-federation` on the [=url/origin=] of its federation's URL. For the
federation `https://atproto.com`, this is `https://atproto.com/.well-known/web-identity-federation`.

<xmp class="idl">
dictionary IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata {
  IdentityProviderBranding branding;
};
</xmp>

<dl dfn-type="dict-member" dfn-for="IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata">
    :   <dfn>branding</dfn>
    ::  The federation's [=federation branding|branding=], in the same format as an [=IDP=]'s
        {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/branding}}: its {{IdentityProviderBranding/name}},
        {{IdentityProviderBranding/icons}}, {{IdentityProviderBranding/background_color}}, and
        {{IdentityProviderBranding/color}}.
</dl>

<div class="example" id="example-operator-metadata">
The operator metadata at `https://atproto.com/.well-known/web-identity-federation`:

```json
{
  "branding": {
    "name": "Atmosphere",
    "icons": [{"url": "https://atproto.com/icon.png", "size": 64}],
    "background_color": "#1185fe",
    "color": "#ffffff"
  }
}
```

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>fetch the operator metadata</dfn> given a [=string=] |federation| and a |globalObject|, run
the following steps. This returns an {{IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata}} or failure.

1. Let |federationUrl| be the result of running the [=URL parser=] on |federation|. If
    |federationUrl| is failure, or its [=url/scheme=] is not "`https`", return failure.
1. Let |url| be a new [=/URL=] with [=url/scheme=] "`https`", [=url/host=] |federationUrl|'s
    [=url/host=], [=url/port=] |federationUrl|'s [=url/port=], and [=url/path=]
    « "`.well-known`", "`web-identity-federation`" ».
1. Run the steps of [=fetch the config file=] that fetch the [=config file=] and convert it to an
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}}, with |url| in place of the config URL, |globalObject|, and
    {{IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata}} in place of {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}}. Return the
    result.

</div>

Note: Like the [=config file=], the [=operator metadata=] is fetched without credentials, without
a referrer, and from an opaque origin, so the [=federation operator=] doesn't learn who is asking
or which [=RP=] they are visiting. User agents MAY cache it according to HTTP caching rules.

Issue: Because the [=operator metadata=] lives at a well-known path on the federation URL's
[=url/origin=], one origin can operate only one federation. Should the federation URL point at
the [=operator metadata=] directly instead?

## Branding ## {#branding}

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>get the federation branding</dfn> given a {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}
|federation| and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns an
{{IdentityProviderBranding}} or null.

1. Let |metadata| be the result of [=fetch the operator metadata=] with |federation| and
    |globalObject|.
1. If |metadata| is failure, or |metadata|["{{IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata/branding}}"]
    doesn't [=map/exist=], return null.
1. Return |metadata|["{{IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata/branding}}"].

</div>

A federation's <dfn>federation branding</dfn> is the {{IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata/branding}}
in its [=operator metadata=], which the user agent obtains with [=get the federation branding=]. The
user agent uses it to show the user which federation an [=RP=] is asking for
([[#account-selection]]), the same
way [[FEDCM]] uses an [=IDP=]'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/branding}}: it uses the
{{IdentityProviderBranding/name}} as the federation's name, picks an icon from
{{IdentityProviderBranding/icons}}, and MAY use {{IdentityProviderBranding/background_color}} and
{{IdentityProviderBranding/color}}, subject to the same contrast requirements.

Note: This addresses [issue 29](https://github.com/w3c-fedid/idp-registration/issues/29).

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# The Handle Registry # {#registry}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

This specification depends on the Infra Standard. [[!INFRA]]

[[HVP]] defines the [=/handles=] that the user agent remembers, how they are parsed and resolved,
and the [=/issuer metadata=] of the [=IDPs=] that issue them. This specification uses the following
terms for them:

* A <dfn>handle</dfn> is an [=HVP handle=]: a domain handle, such as `@alice.example`, in its
    [=HVP normalized form=]. The user
    agent displays it with a leading `@`. A handle isn't necessarily permanent: a user can change
    it, or move it to another [=IDP=].
* An <dfn>issuer</dfn> is the [=IDP=] that is the [=authorized issuer=] of a [=/handle=],
    identified by its [=HVP issuer identifier=], an `https:` [=/origin=].
* To <dfn>parse a handle</dfn> given a [=string=] |input|, run the steps of [=HVP parsing=] on
    |input|, and return the resulting [=/handle=], or failure if they fail.
* Two [=strings=] <dfn lt="handles match">match as handles</dfn> if [=parse a handle|parsing=]
    each of them succeeds and produces the same [=/handle=]. This is the only comparison used on
    handles. It is applied to an account's {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}}, and never to its
    {{IdentityProviderAccount/username}} or {{IdentityProviderAccount/email}}.
* The <dfn>issuer metadata</dfn> of an [=/issuer=] is its [=HVP issuer metadata=], served at its
    [=HVP issuer identifier=] followed by `/.well-known/handle-verification`. It lists the
    federations that the [=/issuer=]'s accounts belong to in its
    [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] member, and the endpoint that issues [=HVTs=] in its
    [=HVP issuer metadata/issuance_endpoint=] member. To <dfn>fetch the issuer metadata</dfn> of
    an [=/issuer=], the user agent fetches and checks it as defined by [[HVP]], without
    credentials, without a referrer, and from an opaque origin.
* <dfn lt="resolve a handle">Resolution</dfn> runs [=HVP resolution=] on a [=/handle=], and produces
    a <dfn>resolution result</dfn> or failure. A [=resolution result=] has the following items:

    <dl dfn-for="resolution result">
        :   <dfn>issuer</dfn>
        ::  The [=HVP issuer identifier=] of the handle's [=/issuer=].
        :   <dfn>unique user identifier</dfn>
        ::  The [=/unique user identifier=] that the handle resolves to: a DID.
    </dl>

<div algorithm="resolve a handle">
To [=resolve a handle=] given a [=/handle=] |handle| and a <var ignore>globalObject</var>, run the steps of
[=HVP resolution=] on |handle|. If they fail, return failure. Otherwise, return a
[=resolution result=] whose [=resolution result/issuer=] is the [=authorized issuer=] that they
produced, and whose [=resolution result/unique user identifier=] is the
[=/unique user identifier=] that they produced.

</div>


Each [=user agent=] has a global, persistent <dfn>handle registry</dfn>, an initially empty
[=ordered set=] of [=/handle|handles=] that the user uses to sign in. No two
[=/handle|handles=] in the [=handle registry=] [=handles match|match=].

A [=/handle=] is added to the [=handle registry=] in the same way whether the [=IDP=] registered it
with {{IdentityProvider/register()}} or the user typed it ([=type your handle=]). The user agent
doesn't record which one did.

The [=handle registry=] stores only [=/handle|handles=]. It doesn't store which [=IDP=] a
[=/handle=] resolves to, that [=IDP=]'s [=/issuer metadata=], or any accounts. The user agent
computes the [=IDP=] from the [=/handle=] by [=resolve a handle|resolving=] it whenever it needs
it, and obtains accounts from the [=IDP=] when needed.

Note: A user agent can cache the results of [=resolve a handle|resolution=] and the documents
that [=create a registered provider=] fetches, for example to avoid lookups and fetches on every
[=federation request=]. That's an implementation choice, as long as the cache respects DNS TTLs
and HTTP caching, follows the caching rules of [[HVP]], and is cleared along with the
[=handle registry=]. See also [[#privacy]] and [[#security]].

When the user clears browsing data or site settings for an [=/origin=] |origin|, the user agent
MUST [=list/remove=] from the [=handle registry=] every [=/handle=] whose [=/issuer=], as determined
by [=resolve a handle=] (or by the user agent's cached result of it), is [=same site=] with
|origin|.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>add to the handle registry</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle|, run the following steps:

1. If the [=handle registry=] contains a [=/handle=] that [=handles match|matches=] |handle|,
    return.
1. [=set/Append=] |handle| to the [=handle registry=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>remove from the handle registry</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle|, [=list/remove=] from
the [=handle registry=] every [=/handle=] that [=handles match|matches=] |handle|.

</div>

The user agent SHOULD let the user see the [=handle registry=] and remove [=/handle|handles=] from
it (by running [=remove from the handle registry=]), for example to remove a mistyped handle.

Note: The [=handle registry=] is deliberately separate from the [[LOGIN-STATUS|login status]] (and
from the [[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY|account registry]], when supported). The login status records whether
the user is signed in right now, which changes silently, while the [=handle registry=] records
which [=/handle|handles=] the user uses, which the user approved and which outlives their session.
After the user signs out, the [=IDP=] of a registered [=/handle=] is still offered, the same way
[[FEDCM]] offers an [=IDP=] that the user is [=logged-out=] of, so the user can sign back in. So an
[=IDP=] calls `setStatus("logged-out")` when the user signs out, and
{{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} only when the [=/handle=] should no longer be offered, for
example when the account is deleted.

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# IdP-initiated Registration # {#registration}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

This section defines how an [=IDP=] registers and unregisters a user's [=/handle=], and what it
declares so that the user agent can present it to [=RPs=] later ([[#presentation]]).

## Registering a handle ## {#register}

<xmp class="idl">
partial interface IdentityProvider {
  static Promise<undefined> register(USVString handle);
  static Promise<undefined> unregister(USVString handle);
};
</xmp>

An [=IDP=] registering a [=/handle=] and the user typing it ([[#user-registration]]) follow the
same path: both [=resolve a handle|resolve=] it, and both add the same [=/handle=] to the
[=handle registry=].

Note: An [=IDP=] can only register and unregister [=/handle|handles=] that it is the [=/issuer=] of,
as named by the records that [=HVP resolution=] reads: the `HandleIssuer` service of the handle's
DID document.

<div algorithm>
A |globalObject| is <dfn>on the issuer's site</dfn> of a [=resolution result=] |result| if
|globalObject|'s [=associated Document=]'s [=Document/origin=] is not an [=opaque origin=], and is
[=same site=] with |result|'s [=resolution result/issuer=].

</div>

<div algorithm="register">
When the static <dfn method for="IdentityProvider">register(|handle|)</dfn> method is invoked, run
the following steps:

1. Let |globalObject| be the [=current global object=].
1. Let |promise| be a [=new=] {{Promise}} in |globalObject|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. If |globalObject|'s [=Window/navigable=] is not a [=top-level traversable=], [=reject=] |promise|
    with a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}} and return |promise|.
1. [=In parallel=], run the following steps:
    1. Let |canonical| be the result of [=parse a handle=] with |handle|. If |canonical| is
        failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{SyntaxError}}" and return.
    1. Let |result| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |canonical| and |globalObject|.
        If |result| is failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NetworkError}}" and return.
    1. If |globalObject| is not [=on the issuer's site=] of |result|, [=queue to reject=]
        |promise| with "{{NotAllowedError}}" and return.
    1. Let |provider| be the result of [=create a registered provider=] with |result|'s
        [=resolution result/issuer=], « |canonical| », null, and |globalObject|. If |provider| is
        failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NetworkError}}" and return.
    1. If |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer metadata=]'s
        [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] doesn't [=map/exist=] or [=list/is empty=],
        [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NetworkError}}" and return.
    1. Let |accounts| be the result of [=get the registered accounts=] with |provider|'s
        [=registered provider/config=], |provider|, and |globalObject|.
    1. If |accounts| [=list/is empty=], [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NotAllowedError}}"
        and return.

        Note: This fails if the user isn't [=logged-in=] to the [=IDP=], if fetching
        {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} fails, or if none of the returned accounts
        has |canonical| as its {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}}.
    1. If the [=handle registry=] doesn't already contain a [=/handle=] that
        [=handles match|matches=] |canonical|, the user agent MUST
        [=ask to save a handle|ask the user to save=] |canonical| with |accounts|[0].
        If the user declines, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NotAllowedError}}" and return.
    1. [=Add to the handle registry=] with |canonical|.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=DOM manipulation task source=] given |globalObject| to
        [=resolve=] |promise|.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>ask to save a handle</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle| and an {{IdentityProviderAccount}}
|account|, the user agent shows a prompt that asks the user whether
to save |handle| so that it's offered when websites ask them to sign in. It returns whether the
user agreed.

* The prompt SHOULD show |handle| as the account's identifier.
* The prompt SHOULD show |account|'s {{IdentityProviderAccount/name}} and
    {{IdentityProviderAccount/picture}}.
* The user agent MAY require [=transient activation=] before showing the prompt, MAY rate-limit
    it, and MAY remember that the user declined and not ask again.

</div>

Note: An [=IDP=] sets its login status to [=logged-in=] before calling
{{IdentityProvider/register()}}, as in the <a href="#example-idp-register">introduction's
example</a>. This way, an [=IDP=] can only register a [=/handle=] for an account the user is
signed in to, and the prompt can show that account's name and picture.

Note: {{IdentityProvider/register()}} is separate from {{NavigatorLogin/setStatus()}} because a
registered [=/handle=] needs to outlive the user's session. See [[#registry]].

<div algorithm="unregister">
When the static <dfn method for="IdentityProvider">unregister(|handle|)</dfn> method is invoked,
run the following steps:

1. Let |globalObject| be the [=current global object=].
1. Let |promise| be a [=new=] {{Promise}} in |globalObject|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. [=In parallel=], run the following steps:
    1. Let |canonical| be the result of [=parse a handle=] with |handle|. If |canonical| is
        failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{SyntaxError}}" and return.
    1. Let |result| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |canonical| and |globalObject|.
        If |result| is failure, [=queue to reject=] |promise| with "{{NetworkError}}" and return.
    1. If |globalObject| is not [=on the issuer's site=] of |result|, [=queue to reject=]
        |promise| with "{{NotAllowedError}}" and return.
    1. [=Remove from the handle registry=] with |canonical|.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=DOM manipulation task source=] given |globalObject| to
        [=resolve=] |promise|.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

{{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} removes the [=/handle=] whether the [=IDP=] registered it or the
user typed it: either way, the handle's [=/issuer=] has said it should no longer be offered. It resolves
the same way whether or not the [=/handle=] was in the [=handle registry=], so that it doesn't
reveal whether the user had registered it.

Note: If a [=/handle=] no longer resolves to the caller, for example because its DID document no
longer names the caller as its issuer, the caller can't unregister it. It doesn't need to,
because the [=/handle=] is no longer offered with the caller as its [=/issuer=] either
([=gather registered providers=] resolves it again).

## Account handles ## {#account-handles}

<xmp class="idl">
partial dictionary IdentityProviderAccount {
  USVString handle;
};
</xmp>

<dl dfn-type="dict-member" dfn-for="IdentityProviderAccount">
    :   <dfn>handle</dfn>
    ::  The account's [=/handle=], for example `"@alice.example"`. The user agent uses it
        to match the account with registered and typed [=/handle|handles=]
        ([=find matching accounts=]), and uses it in the [=HVT request=] ([[#hvt-issuance]]).
</dl>

Note: This is separate from {{IdentityProviderAccount/username}}, which an [=IDP=] can use for
any display name it likes. An account can have both.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>find matching accounts</dfn> given a [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderAccount}}s |accounts|
and a [=/handle=] |handle|, return the [=list/items=] of |accounts| whose
{{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} [=map/exists=] and [=handles match|matches=] |handle|.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
Change the definition of [=displayable=] so that an account with its
{{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} set is also [=displayable=].

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# User-initiated Registration # {#user-registration}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

A user whose [=/handle=] isn't in the [=handle registry=] yet can register it by typing it. The
user agent decides when to allow users to [=type your handle=], for example when an [=RP=] makes a
[=federation request=] ([[#account-selection]]). The user agent
[=check a typed handle|checks=] that the typed handle [=resolve a handle|resolves=] to an
[=/issuer=] that belongs to one of the federations the caller accepts, and adds it to the
[=handle registry=].

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>type your handle</dfn> given a [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} URLs
|federations| and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns a [=/handle=] or null.

1. Show UI that asks the user to type a handle. The user agent MAY offer suggestions from its own
    local data (such as the [=/handle|handles=] in the [=handle registry=]). It MUST NOT
    send network requests while the user types.
1. Wait for the user to submit a [=string=] |input|, or to cancel. If the user cancels, return
    null.
1. Let |handle| be the result of [=check a typed handle=] with |input|, |federations|, and
    |globalObject|.
1. If |handle| is failure, tell the user that no account provider was found for |input|, and go
    back to the first step.
1. [=Add to the handle registry=] with |handle|.

    Note: Submitting the [=/handle=] has the same effect as agreeing to
    [=ask to save a handle|save it=] after {{IdentityProvider/register()}}. The choice is stored
    before sign-in, so that it's remembered even if sign-in fails or the user abandons it.
1. Return |handle|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>check a typed handle</dfn> given a [=string=] |input|, a [=list=] of
{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} URLs |federations|, and a |globalObject|, run the following
steps. This returns a [=/handle=] or failure.

1. Let |handle| be the result of [=parse a handle=] with |input|. If |handle| is failure, return
    failure.
1. Let |result| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |handle| and |globalObject|. If
    |result| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |provider| be the result of [=create a registered provider=] with |result|'s
    [=resolution result/issuer=], « |handle| », null, and |globalObject|. If |provider| is failure,
    return failure.
1. If |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer metadata=]'s
    [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] doesn't [=map/exist=], or none of its
    [=list/items=] is [=list/contained=] in |federations|, return failure.
1. Return |handle|.

</div>

Note: [=Type your handle=] doesn't deal with accounts or with the [=RP=]'s request. It only
registers a [=/handle=]. Showing the handle's accounts and signing in with it is up to the caller
([[#account-selection]]).

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Presentation # {#presentation}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

This section defines how the user agent presents registered [=IDPs=] to the user when an [=RP=]
calls `navigator.credentials.get()` with a {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}, and what the
[=RP=] receives.

## Requesting by federation ## {#request-by-federation}

<xmp class="idl">
partial dictionary IdentityProviderConfig {
  USVString federation;
};
</xmp>

<dl dfn-type="dict-member" dfn-for="IdentityProviderConfig">
    :   <dfn>federation</dfn>
    ::  A URL that identifies a federation the [=RP=] accepts, for example
        `"https://atproto.com"`. When present, {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} is ignored,
        and the entry stands for every registered [=/issuer=] whose [=/issuer metadata=] lists this
        federation in its [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=].
</dl>

An {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} whose {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} [=map/exists=] is a
<dfn>federation request</dfn>. The [=RP=] passes the nonce that the [=HVT+KB=] is bound to as its
{{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/nonce}}.

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [[FEDCM]], remove the `required` keyword from the {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} and
{{IdentityProviderConfig/clientId}} members of {{IdentityProviderConfig}}.

Every algorithm in [[FEDCM]] that reads {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}} from an
{{IdentityProviderConfig}} passed by the [=RP=] MUST first check that the member [=map/exists=],
and treat its absence as failure. The one exception is [=create an IdentityCredential=], as
modified below.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=create an IdentityCredential=], insert the following steps immediately after the step
"Let <var ignore>mediation</var> be <var ignore>options</var>'s {{CredentialRequestOptions/mediation}}":

1. Let |federationRequests| be the [=list/items=] of |providerList| that are [=federation requests=].
1. If |federationRequests|'s [=list/size=] is greater than 1, return (failure, true).
1. [=list/Remove=] the [=list/items=] of |federationRequests| from |providerList|.
1. Let |federationRequest| be |federationRequests|[0] if it exists, and null otherwise.
1. Let |federationBranding| be null.
1. If |federationRequest| is not null:
    1. Let |registered| be the result of [=gather registered providers=] with |federationRequest|,
        |providerList|, and |globalObject|.
    1. [=list/Extend=] |providerList| with |registered|.
    1. If |providerList|'s [=list/size=] is greater than 1, set the
        [=registered provider/offered as a choice=] flag of each [=list/item=] of |registered|.
    1. Set |federationBranding| to the result of [=get the federation branding=] with
        |federationRequest|.{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} and |globalObject|.

    Note: The user agent can fetch the [=operator metadata=] in parallel with
    [=gather registered providers=]. |federationBranding| is used when building the UI
    ([[#account-selection]]).

</div>

## Registered providers ## {#registered-providers}

A <dfn>registered provider</dfn> is an {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} that the user agent
creates for the [=/issuer=] of one or more [=/handle|handles=], rather than one the [=RP=] passed.
It has no {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}. The user agent keeps the following associated with
it:

<dl dfn-for="registered provider">
    :   <dfn>issuer</dfn>
    ::  The [=HVP issuer identifier=] of its [=/issuer=].
    :   <dfn>issuer metadata</dfn>
    ::  The [=/issuer=]'s [=/issuer metadata=], from [=fetch the issuer metadata=].
    :   <dfn>config</dfn>
    ::  The [=/issuer=]'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}}, from [=fetch the issuer config=]. It has
        only an {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} and a
        {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}.
    :   <dfn>handles</dfn>
    ::  A [=list=] of [=/handle|handles=] that [=resolve a handle|resolve=] to this [=/issuer=]:
        the ones in the [=handle registry=], or the single [=/handle=] the user typed or the
        [=IDP=] is registering.
    :   <dfn>accounts</dfn>
    ::  A [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderAccount}}s: the [=get the registered accounts|accounts=]
        that [=handles match|match=] one of its [=registered provider/handles=].
    :   <dfn>offered as a choice</dfn>
    ::  A flag, initially unset. It is set when the request has more than one [=IDP=], so that the
        user chooses which [=IDP=] to sign in to, rather than the user agent opening the login
        dialog of each [=IDP=] the user is [=logged-out=] of.
</dl>

To show the user's accounts and to let the user sign in, the user agent needs the
[=/issuer=]'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} and
{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}. It reads them from the [=well-known file=] of [[FEDCM]] on
the [=/issuer=]'s site, rather than from a [=config file=]: a [=registered provider=] has no config
URL.

<div class="example" id="example-well-known">
The [=well-known file=] at `https://social.example/.well-known/web-identity`:

```json
{
  "accounts_endpoint": "/accounts",
  "login_url": "/login"
}
```

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>fetch the issuer config</dfn> given an [=HVP issuer identifier=] |issuer| and a
|globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns an {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}} or failure.

1. Let |site| be the [=host/registrable domain=] of |issuer|'s [=origin/host=]. If |site| is null,
    return failure.
1. Let |wellKnownUrl| be a new [=/URL=] whose [=url/scheme=] is "`https`", [=url/host=] is |site|,
    and [=url/path=] is « "`.well-known`", "`web-identity`" ».
1. Let |wellKnown| be the result of [=fetch the well-known file=] with |wellKnownUrl| and
    |globalObject|. If |wellKnown| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |config| be a new {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}}.
1. For each member name |m| of « "`accounts_endpoint`", "`login_url`" »:
    1. If |wellKnown|[|m|] doesn't [=map/exist=], return failure.
    1. Let |url| be the result of running the [=URL parser=] on |wellKnown|[|m|], with
        |wellKnownUrl| as the base URL.
    1. If |url| is failure, or its [=url/scheme=] is not "`https`", or its [=url/origin=] is not
        [=same site=] with |issuer|, return failure.
    1. Set |config|[|m|] to the [=URL serializer|serialization=] of |url|.
1. Return |config|.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [[FEDCM]], remove the `required` keyword from the {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/id_assertion_endpoint}}
member of {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}}. A [=registered provider=] doesn't use it
([[#hvt-issuance]]). Every algorithm in [[FEDCM]] that reads it from the [=config file=] of an
[=IDP=] that isn't a [=registered provider=] MUST first check that the member [=map/exists=], and
treat its absence as failure.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
A [=registered provider=] has no {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}. In [[FEDCM]]:

* In [=fetch the config file=], if |provider| is a [=registered provider=], return its
    [=registered provider/config=], without fetching anything.
* Wherever an algorithm uses the [=url/origin=] of a provider's
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}, for example to get its login status, it uses the
    [=registered provider/issuer=] of a [=registered provider=] instead.
* The {{IdentityCredential/configURL}} of an {{IdentityCredential}} created for a
    [=registered provider=] is the empty string. The [=RP=] identifies the [=/issuer=] from the
    `iss` claim of the [=HVT=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>create a registered provider</dfn> given an [=HVP issuer identifier=] |issuer|, a [=list=]
of [=/handle|handles=] |handles|, an {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} or null
|federationRequest|, and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns an
{{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} or failure.

1. Let |metadata| be the result of [=fetch the issuer metadata=] with |issuer| and |globalObject|.
    If |metadata| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |config| be the result of [=fetch the issuer config=] with |issuer| and |globalObject|. If
    |config| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |provider| be a new {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}}.
1. If |federationRequest| is not null, then for each member name |m| of «
    {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/nonce}}, {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/loginHint}},
    {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/domainHint}} »: if |federationRequest|[|m|] [=map/exists=],
    set |provider|[|m|] to |federationRequest|[|m|].
1. Mark |provider| as a [=registered provider=], and set its [=registered provider/issuer=] to
    |issuer|, its [=registered provider/issuer metadata=] to |metadata|, its
    [=registered provider/config=] to |config|, and its [=registered provider/handles=] to
    |handles|.
1. Return |provider|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>get the registered accounts</dfn> given an {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig}} |config|, a
[=registered provider=] |provider|, and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns a
[=list=] of {{IdentityProviderAccount}}s.

1. If [=get the login status=] for |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer=] is not
    [=logged-in=], return an empty [=list=].
1. Return the result of [=create identity credential/fetch accounts=] with |config|, |provider|,
    and |globalObject|.

    Note: [=create identity credential/fetch accounts=] only returns the accounts that
    [=handles match|match=] one of |provider|'s [=registered provider/handles=] (see below).

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=filter accounts=], insert the following step before the step that returns
<var ignore>accountList</var>:

1. If |provider| is a [=registered provider=], remove from <var ignore>accountList</var> every
    account whose {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} doesn't [=map/exist=] or doesn't
    [=handles match|match=] one of |provider|'s [=registered provider/handles=].

Note: This filters the accounts the same way as {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/loginHint}}. Like
the other filters, it runs after the account pictures are fetched, so that the [=IDP=] can't tell
from the picture fetches which accounts were kept.

</div>

Note: Only accounts whose {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} is a registered [=/handle=] are shown
to other [=RPs=], because those are the accounts the user agreed to (with
{{IdentityProvider/register()}}, or by typing the [=/handle=]). An [=IDP=]'s
{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} can return other accounts (or push them via
[[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY]]), but they aren't shown on a [=federation request=] until their
[=/handle|handles=] are registered.

Issue: Should other accounts from a registered [=IDP=] also be shown, once the user has
registered at least one [=/handle=] with it?

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>group registered handles by issuer</dfn> given a |globalObject|, run the following steps.
This returns an [=ordered map=] from [=HVP issuer identifiers=] to [=lists=] of
[=/handle|handles=].

1. Let |groups| be an empty [=ordered map=].
1. For each |handle| of the [=handle registry=]:
    1. Let |result| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |handle| and |globalObject|. If
        |result| is failure, [=iteration/continue=].
    1. Let |issuer| be |result|'s [=resolution result/issuer=].
    1. If |groups|[|issuer|] doesn't [=map/exist=], [=map/set=] |groups|[|issuer|] to an empty
        [=list=].
    1. [=list/Append=] |handle| to |groups|[|issuer|].
1. Return |groups|.

</div>

Note: Several [=/handle|handles=] can resolve to the same [=/issuer=], for example when two users
of the same [=IDP=] share a user agent. Grouping them means the user agent fetches that
[=/issuer=]'s [=/issuer metadata=] and accounts once.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>gather registered providers</dfn> given an {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}}
|federationRequest|, a [=list=] of {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions}} |explicitProviders|, and a
|globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns a [=list=] of [=registered providers=].

1. Let |seenOrigins| be the [=ordered set=] of the [=url/origins=] of the
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/configURL}}s of |explicitProviders|.
1. Let |result| be an empty [=list=].
1. For each |issuer| → |handles| of the result of [=group registered handles by issuer=] with
    |globalObject|:
    1. If |seenOrigins| [=list/contains=] |issuer|, [=iteration/continue=].

        Note: If the [=RP=] also lists this [=IDP=] explicitly, the explicit entry is used and
        the registration is ignored, so that the same [=IDP=] doesn't appear twice.
    1. Let |provider| be the result of [=create a registered provider=] with |issuer|, |handles|,
        |federationRequest|, and |globalObject|. If |provider| is failure, [=iteration/continue=].
    1. If |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer metadata=]'s
        [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] doesn't [=map/exist=] or doesn't
        [=list/contain=] |federationRequest|.{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}},
        [=iteration/continue=].
    1. [=list/Append=] |provider| to |result|.
1. Return |result|.

</div>

Note: [=Gather registered providers=] fetches each [=/issuer=]'s [=/issuer metadata=] and
[=well-known file=], without credentials, but not its accounts. [=Create an IdentityCredential=]
then fetches each [=registered provider=]'s accounts like any other [=IDP=]'s: the user agent checks its login status, fetches its accounts (keeping only the
accounts that [=handles match|match=] its [=registered provider/handles=]), and shows the same UI
as [[FEDCM]] does when the user is [=logged-out=] or when no account matches.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>load a registered provider</dfn> given a [=registered provider=] |provider|, a
[=federation request=] |federationRequest|, and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This
returns success or failure.

1. If |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer metadata=]'s
    [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] doesn't [=map/exist=] or doesn't
    [=list/contain=] |federationRequest|.{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}}, return failure.
1. Set |provider|'s [=registered provider/accounts=] to the result of
    [=get the registered accounts=] with |provider|'s [=registered provider/config=], |provider|,
    and |globalObject|.
1. Return success.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=handle a logged-out IDP=], insert the following step at the beginning:

1. If |provider| is a [=registered provider=] whose [=registered provider/offered as a choice=]
    flag is set, return "logged-out".

In [=create an IdentityCredential=], in the "Build UI" step, when the value for a
[=registered provider=] is "logged-out" and <var ignore>mode</var> is
{{IdentityCredentialRequestOptionsMode/active}}, the user agent MUST prompt the user whether to
continue with this [=IDP=], rather than adding nothing or returning (failure, false).

Note: When a request has more than one [=IDP=], each [=IDP=] that the user is [=logged-out=] of is
then offered as a choice next to the accounts of the [=IDPs=] the user is signed in to, and only
the one the user chooses opens its login dialog. With a single [=IDP=], [[FEDCM]]'s behavior is
unchanged.

</div>

## Account selection ## {#account-selection}

A <dfn>handle chip</dfn> for a [=/handle=] |handle|, a [=registered provider=] |provider|, and an
{{IdentityProviderBranding}} or null |federationBranding| is a user agent UI element that
represents an account the user hasn't signed in to yet:

* Its primary label is |handle|, displayed with a leading `@`.
* If |federationBranding| is not null, it SHOULD show it, as described in [[#branding]]: for
    example, the federation's icon, and its {{IdentityProviderBranding/name}} as secondary text.
    Otherwise, it uses a generic icon.
* It MAY show the [=origin/host=] of |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer=] as secondary
    text, so that the user knows where they'll sign in.

An account from [=find matching accounts=] is shown the same way FedCM shows accounts. If
|federationBranding| is not null, the user agent MAY add its icon as a badge.

The user agent MAY also use |federationBranding| elsewhere in the dialog, for example to title it
with the federation's {{IdentityProviderBranding/name}} ("Sign in with your Atmosphere account"),
and in the UI that allows users to [=type your handle=].

<div algorithm="sign in with a handle">
To <dfn>sign in with a handle</dfn> given a [=/handle=] |handle|, a [=registered provider=]
|provider|, and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns an
{{IdentityProviderAccount}} or failure.

1. Let |loginProvider| be a copy of |provider|. If |loginProvider|'s
    {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/loginHint}} is empty, set it to |handle|.

    Note: [=Show an IDP login dialog=] sends |loginProvider|'s
    {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/loginHint}} to the [=IDP=] as `login_hint`. Only the login
    dialog uses |loginProvider|, so that the [=/handle=] doesn't also filter the accounts that are
    fetched afterwards.
1. Let |result| be the result of [=show an IDP login dialog=] with |provider|'s
    [=registered provider/config=], |loginProvider|, and |globalObject|.
1. If |result| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |matches| be the result of [=find matching accounts=] with the result of
    [=get the registered accounts=] with |provider|'s [=registered provider/config=], |provider|,
    and |globalObject|, and with |handle|.
1. If |matches|'s [=list/size=] is 1, return |matches|[0].
1. If |matches|'s [=list/size=] is greater than 1, let the user choose one of |matches| and
    return it. If the user cancels, return failure.
1. Tell the user that the account they signed in with doesn't match |handle|, and return failure.

</div>

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=create an IdentityCredential=]:

Change the step "If |providerMap| [=map/is empty=], return (failure, false)" to:

1. If |providerMap| [=map/is empty=]:
    1. If |federationRequest| is null, or <var ignore>mode</var> is not
        {{IdentityCredentialRequestOptionsMode/active}}, return (failure, false).
    1. Otherwise, the user agent MAY continue, only allowing users to [=type your handle=].

After the "Build UI" step, add the following step:

1. If |federationRequest| is not null, the user agent SHOULD allow users to [=type your handle=].
    If the user chooses to:
    1. Let |handle| be the result of [=type your handle=] with «
        |federationRequest|.{{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} » and |globalObject|. If |handle|
        is null, return to the dialog.
    1. Let |result| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |handle| and |globalObject|. If
        |result| is failure, return to the dialog.
    1. Let |provider| be the result of [=create a registered provider=] with |result|'s
        [=resolution result/issuer=], « |handle| », and |federationRequest|.
    1. If [=load a registered provider=] with |provider|, |federationRequest|, and |globalObject|
        returns failure, return to the dialog.
    1. If |provider|'s [=registered provider/accounts=] is not empty, show each of them.
        Otherwise, show a [=handle chip=] for |handle|, |provider|, and
        |federationBranding|.
    1. Wait for the user to select one of them, or to cancel. If the user cancels, return to the
        dialog.
    1. If the user selected an account, let |account| be that account. Otherwise, let |account|
        be the result of [=sign in with a handle=] with |handle|, |provider|, and |globalObject|,
        and if |account| is failure, return to the dialog.
    1. Set <var ignore>selectedAccount</var> to |account| and <var ignore>permission</var> to true,
        set the relevant provider to |provider| and the relevant config to |provider|'s
        [=registered provider/config=], and close the dialog.

    Note: The user agent can reuse the results of [=resolve a handle|resolution=] and
    [=create a registered provider=] from [=type your handle=], rather than repeating them.

</div>

## Issuing the HVT+KB ## {#hvt-issuance}

When the user selects an account of a [=registered provider=], the [=RP=] receives an [=HVT+KB=]
[[HVP]], rather than the token of [[FEDCM]]'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/id_assertion_endpoint}}.
The user agent obtains an [=HVT=] from the [=/issuer=] with an [=HVT request=], which doesn't
reveal the [=RP=] to the [=/issuer=], and binds it to the [=RP=] itself.

<div class="monkeypatch">
In [=fetch an identity assertion=], insert the following step at the beginning:

1. If |provider| is a [=registered provider=], return the result of [=request an HVT+KB=] with
    |provider|, the account that the user selected, and |globalObject|, as the token.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>request an HVT+KB</dfn> given a [=registered provider=] |provider|, an
{{IdentityProviderAccount}} |account|, and a |globalObject|, run the following steps. This returns
a [=string=] or failure.

1. Let |handle| be the result of [=parse a handle=] with |account|'s
    {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}}. If |handle| is failure, return failure.
1. Let |result| be the result of [=resolve a handle=] with |handle| and |globalObject|. The user
    agent MAY reuse the result of an earlier resolution of |handle|. If |result| is failure, or
    its [=resolution result/issuer=] isn't |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer=], return
    failure.
1. Let |nonce| be |provider|'s {{IdentityProviderRequestOptions/nonce}}. If it doesn't
    [=map/exist=], return failure.
1. Generate a new key pair.
1. Send an [=HVT request=] to |provider|'s [=registered provider/issuer metadata=]'s
    [=HVP issuer metadata/issuance_endpoint=], as defined by [[HVP]], with |handle| and
    |result|'s [=resolution result/unique user identifier=], signed with the key pair. The request
    includes the [=/issuer=]'s cookies, and doesn't include a referrer or reveal
    |globalObject|'s [=/origin=].
1. If the response is an [=HVT error response=], return failure.

    Note: The user agent sends an [=HVT request=] only for an account that it obtained from the
    [=/issuer=]'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}}, which it fetches only when the
    user is [=logged-in=] to the [=/issuer=]. An `authentication_required` error is therefore
    unexpected, and the user agent treats it, like any other error, as failure.
1. Let |hvt| be the [=HVT=] in the response. If it fails [=HVT verification=], including the
    checks that the browser performs, return failure.
1. Return the [=HVT+KB=] formed from |hvt| and a KB-JWT, signed with the key pair, whose `aud` is
    the [=ASCII serialization of an origin|serialization=] of |globalObject|'s
    [=associated Document=]'s [=Document/origin=] and whose `nonce` is |nonce|, as defined by
    [[HVP]].

</div>

Note: The [=RP=] verifies the [=HVT+KB=] as described in [=HVT+KB verification=], including
resolving the [=/handle=] itself. The verification is the same whichever federation the [=RP=]
asked for. The `sub` claim of the [=HVT=] carries the user's
[=resolution result/unique user identifier=], which the [=RP=] uses to identify the account.

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Requirements on other parties # {#requirements}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

*This section is non-normative.*

<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Party<th>What it does
<tbody>
<tr><td>[=IDP=]<td>
    Is the [=authorized issuer=] of its users' [=/handle|handles=], as defined by [[HVP]]. Serves
    its [=/issuer metadata=] at `/.well-known/handle-verification`, including the
    [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=] its accounts belong to. Serves a
    [=well-known file=] at its site that names its {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}}
    and {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}.
    Answers [=HVT requests=] at its [=HVP issuer metadata/issuance_endpoint=]. Sets each
    account's {{IdentityProviderAccount/handle}} to the user's handle in its
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} response (or when pushing accounts with
    [[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY]]). After the user signs in, sets its login status to [=logged-in=] and calls
    {{IdentityProvider/register()}} with that handle. Calls {{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} when
    the handle stops existing (e.g. the account is deleted or renamed), and
    `setStatus("logged-out")` when the user merely signs out. Accepts `login_hint` on its
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}.
<tr><td>Owner of a handle<td>
    Publishes the records that [=HVP resolution=] reads, which name the [=/issuer=]: a DID and a
    DID document with a `HandleIssuer` service.
<tr><td>[=Federation operator=]<td>
    Serves [=operator metadata=] at `/.well-known/web-identity-federation` on the origin of its
    {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} URL, including the federation's
    {{IdentityFederationOperatorMetadata/branding}}.
<tr><td>[=RP=]<td>
    Passes a {{IdentityProviderConfig/federation}} and a nonce, and verifies the returned
    [=HVT+KB=] as described in [=HVT+KB verification=].
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Privacy considerations # {#privacy}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<table class="data">
<thead><tr><th>Request<th>When<th>Credentials<th>Reveals the RP?
<tbody>
<tr><td>[=Operator metadata=] of the requested federation<td>On a [=federation request=], unless
    cached<td>No<td>No
<tr><td>[=HVP resolution=] of each registered [=/handle=]<td>On a [=federation request=], unless
    cached<td>No<td>No
<tr><td>[=/Issuer metadata=] and [=well-known file=] of registered
    [=/issuers=]<td>On a [=federation request=], unless cached<td>No<td>No
<tr><td>{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} of registered [=/issuers=]<td>On a
    [=federation request=], when the [=IDP=]'s login status is [=logged-in=]<td>Yes<td>No
<tr><td>[=HVP resolution=], [=/issuer metadata=], [=well-known file=], and (when [=logged-in=])
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} of the resolved [=/issuer=]<td>After
    the user submits a handle, or when an [=IDP=] calls {{IdentityProvider/register()}} or
    {{IdentityProvider/unregister()}}<td>Only for
    {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}}<td>No
<tr><td>{{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}<td>After the user chooses to sign in to an [=IDP=]
    they are [=logged-out=] of, or selects a [=handle chip=]<td>Yes
    (a top-level navigation)<td>No
<tr><td>[=HVT request=] to the [=HVP issuer metadata/issuance_endpoint=]<td>After the user
    selects an account<td>Yes<td>No
</table>

* Unlike [[FEDCM]]'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/id_assertion_endpoint}}, the [=HVT request=]
    doesn't reveal the [=RP=] to the [=/issuer=]: the user agent binds the [=HVT=] to the [=RP=]'s
    origin and nonce itself, in the KB-JWT, as described in [[HVP]].
* A registered [=IDP=] whose login status is [=logged-out=] receives no credentialed request
    before the user chooses to sign in to it. When the [=IDP=]'s login status is
    [=logged-in=], the user agent fetches its {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} as in
    [[FEDCM]] (unless the [=IDP=] and user agent use [[ACCOUNT-REGISTRY]], which avoids the
    credentialed fetch).
* Typing a handle reveals it to the DNS resolver and to the servers involved in its
    [=HVP resolution=], and signing in with a [=handle chip=] sends the handle to the [=IDP=], but
    nothing is revealed to the [=RP=] until the user finishes signing in.
* Because the [=handle registry=] stores only [=/handle|handles=], a [=federation request=] can cause
    [=HVP resolution=] of the user's registered [=/handle|handles=], which the DNS resolver and the
    servers involved can observe. The requests don't carry credentials or reveal the [=RP=]. User
    agents can reduce this by caching resolution results (see [[#registry]]).
* The [=handle registry=] is global. It is used only in user agent UI, and is never exposed to the
    [=RP=] until the user selects an account.
* The [=promise rejection delay|delay before rejection=] defined in [[FEDCM]] still applies, so an
    [=RP=] can't tell whether the user has any registered [=IDPs=].

See also the privacy considerations of [[HVP]].

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Security considerations # {#security}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

* **Resolution isn't proof, verification is.** An attacker who manipulates the user agent's
    resolution can make it end at an [=IDP=] of their choosing. That gets them the typed handle.
    They don't get the user's credentials for the real [=IDP=], and they can't make the [=RP=]
    accept an [=HVT+KB=] for someone else's handle, because the [=RP=] resolves the handle itself
    during [=HVT+KB verification=]. See the security considerations of [[HVP]].
* **Handles that are mistyped or don't exist** either fail to resolve, or resolve to a provider
    that has no matching account: [=find matching accounts=] returns nothing, and the
    [=handle chip=] shows the handle exactly as typed.
* **An account that doesn't match** isn't used: [=sign in with a handle=] fails if the account the
    user signs in with doesn't match the handle, and the [=/issuer=] doesn't issue an [=HVT=] for a
    handle that the signed-in user doesn't control.
* **Registration prompts** can be abused for spam. User agents can rate-limit
    {{IdentityProvider/register()}}, require [=transient activation=], and remember when the user
    declines.
* **A [=/handle=]'s [=/issuer=] can change.** Because the [=handle registry=] stores only
    [=/handle|handles=], a registered [=/handle=] follows the records that [=HVP resolution=] reads:
    if they change, the [=/handle=] is offered with the new [=/issuer=]. That is how a user moves a
    [=/handle=] to another [=IDP=], but it also means that whoever controls the handle's domain
    later (for example, after the domain expires) controls the handle's [=/issuer=]. A user agent
    that caches resolution results MAY notice such a change and ask the user to confirm it.
* **An [=IDP=] can only register [=/handle|handles=] it is the [=/issuer=] of.**
    {{IdentityProvider/register()}} requires the caller to be [=on the issuer's site=] of the
    handle's [=resolution result=], so a site can't register handles whose [=/issuer=] is another
    [=IDP=]. Similarly, {{IdentityProvider/unregister()}} only removes [=/handle|handles=] that
    resolve to the caller's site.

<!-- ============================================================ -->
# Examples # {#examples}
<!-- ============================================================ -->

*This section is non-normative.*

<div class="example" id="example-domain-handle">
**A domain handle on a self-hosted server.** Alice runs her own atproto server at
`pds.alice.example`, and her handle is `@alice.example`.

1. Alice chooses to [=type your handle=] and types `@alice.example`. [=Parse a handle=] gives the
    domain handle `alice.example`.
1. [=HVP resolution=] finds the DID that `alice.example` publishes, `did:plc:...`, whose DID
    document claims `at://alice.example` back and names `https://pds.alice.example` in its
    `HandleIssuer` service.
1. `https://pds.alice.example/.well-known/handle-verification` lists `https://atproto.com` in its
    [=HVP issuer metadata/federations=], and `https://alice.example/.well-known/web-identity`
    names its accounts endpoint and login URL, so the user agent adds `@alice.example` to the [=handle registry=].
1. Alice isn't signed in to `pds.alice.example` yet, so the user agent shows a [=handle chip=] for
    `@alice.example`.
1. When Alice selects it, the user agent opens `login_url?login_hint=alice.example`. Her server
    signs her in, and its {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} returns an account with
    `{handle: "@alice.example"}`.
1. The user agent sends an [=HVT request=] for `alice.example` and `did:plc:...` to the
    [=HVP issuer metadata/issuance_endpoint=], and returns the [=HVT+KB=] to the [=RP=].

</div>

<div class="example" id="example-shared-host">
**Many users share an issuer.** Dave (`@dave.social.example`, a handle under the server's domain)
and Erin (`@erin.example`, her own domain), whose DID documents both name `https://social.example`
as their issuer, both resolve to the same [=/issuer=].
The [=handle registry=] has both [=/handle|handles=], and [=group registered handles by issuer=]
groups them together. On a [=federation request=], the user agent fetches that [=/issuer metadata=]
and {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/accounts_endpoint}} once, and shows the signed-in accounts that
match `@dave.social.example` or `@erin.example`.

</div>

<div class="example" id="example-lifecycle">
**Signing out versus deleting an account.** Erin registered `@erin.social.example` from
`social.example`.

1. Erin signs out of `social.example`, which calls `navigator.login.setStatus("logged-out")`.
    `@erin.social.example` stays in the [=handle registry=]. On the next
    [=federation request=], the user agent sees that `social.example` is [=logged-out=] and offers it
    as [[FEDCM]] does for an [=IDP=] the user is [=logged-out=] of. Signing in opens
    `social.example`'s {{IdentityProviderAPIConfig/login_url}}.
1. Later, Erin deletes her account. `social.example` calls
    `IdentityProvider.unregister("@erin.social.example")`, and the [=/handle=] is removed from the
    [=handle registry=], so the handle is no longer offered to any [=RP=].

</div>

